  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1140 PM CDT Tue Aug 11 2026\n\nLate evening water vapor satellite imagery and RAP upper air \nanalysis reveal the classic summertime subtropical ridge is spread \nout atop the southern plains, with a belt of modest west-\nnorthwesterlies to the north over the northern plains. At the \nsurface, weak low pressure is centered over eastern CO and adjacent \nareas, resulting in southerly winds across southwest KS. Short range \nensembles suggest the upper level ridge will weaken a bit daytime \nWednesday, allowing westerly momentum across the Rockies to expand \nsouthward and deepening the surface trough in response. Gusty \nsoutherly/south-southwesterly winds will be the consequence, \nproviding a modicum of relief from yet another blisteringly hot \nafternoon as highs reach the 103-108 degree range. Despite weak \nheight falls, all precipitation should remain displaced to the west \nof our area as there is no rest for the weary. Wednesday night, \nsoutherly winds will stay elevated, preventing Thursday morning lows \nfrom dropping below the mid 70s to low 80s for most locations.\n\nDaytime Thursday, ensembles agree the upper ridge will re-strengthen \nand slide eastward to the gulf coast states/Dixie Alley, with 500-mb \nflow above the central plains turning southwesterly. Attendant \nsurface low will drift into southwest KS initially Thursday morning \nbefore settling in central KS during the afternoon. While cool air \non the back side of this feature will be limited, at least a few \ndegrees will be shaved off temperatures across our northwest zones, \nyielding a wide range in afternoon highs with upper 90s northwest to \n105-110 southeast. Thankfully, a weak mid-level disturbance will \neject onto the central plains courtesy of the aforementioned \nsouthwest 500-mb flow, bringing a chance for precipitation favoring \nthe west/northwest zones. NBM pops are mainly in the chance category \n(25-54%) with a few pockets of likely (55-74%). No severe threat is \nexpected with this activity as deep-layer shear will be nowhere near \nsufficient.\n\nFriday through the end of the period, medium range ensembles \nindicate the upper level ridge over the southeast CONUS will be \nstatic through the weekend before retrograding towards the southern \nplains early next business week. This evolution should engender a \nsimilar forecast Friday through Sunday, with afternoon highs \ngenerally ranging from the low/mid 90s northwest to the low 100s \nsoutheast as well as precipitation chances in the evening. The \nretrograding ridge Monday onward however will put a stop to \nprecipitation chances but promote further highs in the 90s to low \n100s.",
